# Cloudflare Pages — Custom HTTP headers
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/
# (Netlify also supports this file format, so it works on both platforms.)
#
# ── THE CONTENT-SECURITY-POLICY IS ENFORCED NOW ─────────────────────────────
#
# It used to be four directives — frame-ancestors, object-src, base-uri,
# upgrade-insecure-requests — with the long, carefully built policy sitting on
# the Report-Only header where it constrained nothing. So nothing stopped a
# script injected into checkout.html from reading the page around the card
# frame and posting it somewhere else. That is the specific control a PCI
# SAQ-A-EP questionnaire asks about, and the answer was "we are thinking about
# it".
#
# WHAT HAD TO BE FIXED BEFORE IT COULD BE TURNED ON. The report-only policy was
# not correct — it had never been enforced, so nothing had ever caught its
# gaps. Three script hosts the site genuinely uses were missing from it, found
# by walking every browser-side file for both static <script src> and
# dynamically assigned .src (the second is where the third-party SDKs live, and
# a scan that only reads HTML misses all of them):
#
#     www.paypal.com      paypal-button.js — the PayPal SDK.  Promoting the
#                         policy as written would have broken PayPal checkout.
#     cdn.jsdelivr.net    admin-finance.js (Chart.js), builder.html (QRious)
#     unpkg.com           analytics.html (React UMD builds)
#
# tests/csp-is-enforced-and-complete.test.js now walks the same files on every
# run, so the next SDK somebody adds fails in CI rather than on checkout.
#
# WHAT IS STILL LOOSE, DELIBERATELY. script-src keeps 'unsafe-inline' and
# 'unsafe-eval'. Every page in this repository has inline handlers and inline
# pre-paint blocks, so removing it is a real piece of work — hashes or a nonce
# on every one of them — and shipping that in the same change as the promotion
# would mean a broken storefront and no way to tell which half did it.
#
# So the enforced policy stops a script being LOADED from a host nobody
# allowlisted, which is how skimmers actually arrive, and does not yet stop one
# being injected inline. That is a real improvement and a partial one, and it
# is written down rather than implied.
#
# ── THERE IS NO REPORT-ONLY HEADER, AND THAT IS THE SECOND CORRECTION ───────
#
# One shipped alongside this: the same policy WITHOUT 'unsafe-inline', so that
# /api/csp-report would collect the list of inline blocks needing a hash before
# the next tightening. Sound in principle. Measured on the home page:
#
#     enforced only              82 requests
#     enforced + report-only    100 requests     18 CSP violations per load
#
# One report per inline block, per page view, from every visitor, for ever —
# and each one is a POST that goes through the rate limiter and a Supabase
# write. It bought a list that does not change between page views.
#
# The same list, read out of the repository in a second and costing nobody
# anything:
#
#     134 inline <script> blocks site-wide
#      17 blocks + 17 inline handlers on index.html — which is exactly the 18
#         violations that were being reported on every single load
#     230 inline handlers on admin.html alone
#
# That is the scope of removing 'unsafe-inline', and it did not need telemetry.
# The report-uri stays on the ENFORCED policy, where a report means something
# actually broke — rare, and worth hearing about.
#
# ── 2000 CHARACTERS A LINE, AND OVER IT THE LINE IS SILENTLY DROPPED ────────
#
# The first version of this shipped at 2227 and 2182 characters. Cloudflare
# Pages did not warn, did not fail the build, and did not truncate — it dropped
# BOTH lines, so the deployed site served no Content-Security-Policy at all.
# Worse than before the change, which at least had frame-ancestors. Measured on
# the deployed response: every other header in this block was present and the
# two CSP lines were simply absent.
#
# The old report-only line was 1946 characters and had always been delivered,
# which is what pins the limit at 2000 rather than leaving it a guess.
#
# GETTING UNDER IT WITHOUT GIVING ANYTHING UP:
#
#   scheme prefixes dropped   A host-source with no scheme matches the
#                             document's own scheme or better. This document is
#                             always https (HSTS + upgrade-insecure-requests),
#                             so `*.stripe.com` here means exactly what
#                             `https://*.stripe.com` meant, at 8 fewer
#                             characters a host across ~55 hosts. wss:// IS
#                             kept explicit on the chat widgets: the spec says
#                             an https document matches wss on a bare host, and
#                             a live chat that silently stops connecting is not
#                             the place to lean on that.
#   wildcards collapsed       *.stripe.com already covered js.stripe.com and
#                             hooks.stripe.com; *.paypal.com covered
#                             www.paypal.com; *.google-analytics.com covered
#                             www.google-analytics.com.
#
# tests/csp-is-enforced-and-complete.test.js now measures every line in this
# file against the limit, so this cannot ship again.
#
# ── ONE HOST THE FIRST VERSION WOULD HAVE BROKEN ────────────────────────────
#
# media-src did not include images.zuwera.store, and that is where R2 serves
# product media — admin-main.js filters that host for rows with
# media_type === 'video'. 'self' does NOT cover a subdomain, and unlike img-src
# there is no bare `https:` in media-src to catch it, so every R2-hosted product
# video would have stopped playing the moment the policy took effect. It did not
# take effect, because of the length bug above. Two mistakes cancelling is not a
# defence; media-src now names *.zuwera.store.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: payment=(*)
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'unsafe-eval' *.stripe.com *.paypal.com cdn.jsdelivr.net unpkg.com www.googletagmanager.com connect.facebook.net *.posthog.com static.cloudflareinsights.com www.googleadservices.com googleads.g.doubleclick.net www.clarity.ms browser.sentry-cdn.com *.crisp.chat embed.tawk.to s.pinimg.com analytics.tiktok.com plausible.io widget.trustpilot.com challenges.cloudflare.com; connect-src 'self' *.supabase.co *.stripe.com *.paypal.com *.posthog.com *.google-analytics.com analytics.google.com *.facebook.com cloudflareinsights.com api.github.com www.googletagmanager.com *.doubleclick.net *.clarity.ms *.ingest.sentry.io *.crisp.chat wss://*.crisp.chat *.tawk.to wss://*.tawk.to ct.pinterest.com analytics.tiktok.com plausible.io challenges.cloudflare.com www.google.com www.googleadservices.com; media-src 'self' *.zuwera.store *.supabase.co *.r2.dev res.cloudinary.com blob: data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' fonts.googleapis.com *.crisp.chat embed.tawk.to; font-src 'self' data: fonts.gstatic.com *.crisp.chat; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; frame-src 'self' *.stripe.com *.paypal.com *.facebook.com www.googletagmanager.com td.doubleclick.net widget.trustpilot.com *.tawk.to www.youtube.com www.youtube-nocookie.com player.vimeo.com www.tiktok.com www.instagram.com challenges.cloudflare.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; form-action 'self' *.paypal.com *.stripe.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'self'; upgrade-insecure-requests; report-uri /api/csp-report
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate

# Allow analytics.html to be embedded as an iframe within the same site (admin dashboard)
# Both rules needed — CF Pages serves analytics.html at /analytics (pretty URL) too
/analytics
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN

/analytics.html
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN

# Allow sizeguide to be embedded as an iframe in the product page size modal
# CF Pages serves sizeguide.html at /sizeguide (pretty URL) — both rules needed
/sizeguide
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN

/sizeguide.html
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN

/*.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate

/*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

# Build id for the client auto-reload — must never be cached so an open tab can
# detect a new deploy (version-check in header-scroll.js).
/version.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

/*.woff2
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/*.woff
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Fonts are versioned + never change under a URL → cache hard like js/css.
/*.ttf
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/*.otf
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Vector/icon assets — small, effectively static; a month's cache, revalidate after.
/*.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/*.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

# Raster images + video — a month's cache. NOT immutable on purpose: image URLs are
# hand-versioned (?v=N), not auto-hashed like js/css, so a revalidate-after-a-month
# window means a swapped image can never be stuck stale indefinitely.
/*.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/*.jpg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/*.jpeg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/*.webp
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/*.avif
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/*.gif
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/*.mp4
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/*.webm
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/*.mov
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

# Apple Pay domain verification file (required by Stripe / Apple Pay)
/.well-known/apple-developer-merchantid-domain-association
  Content-Type: application/octet-stream
  Cache-Control: no-cache
